    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay questions the common narrative used to explain rural outmigration—economic decline—which, the author argues, overshadows the psychological and social factors that also contribute to the outmigration of academically talented youth. The author then shows the ways the education system perpetuates these factors, primarily through the deficit thinking teachers hold about rural communities coupled with standardized curricula that may “dis-embed” students from their homes and effectively prime them to leave.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
